.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1208 AM PDT Wed Sep 16 2026
(Today and tonight)

Pretty easy to discern the longwave pattern over the region by
taking a peek at the water vapor imagery. A wound up upper level
low is digging over OR/CA border. The band of upper level clouds
that streamed over the Bay Area the last two days has been shunted
southward. Switching to a different satellite spectral band
quickly shows what`s happening at the surface as a result of the
upper level pattern. Satellite fog product (difference of the
10.3-3.9 um IR bands) reveals decent stratus/marine layer
coverage along the coast and pushing into the inland valleys. Per
observational data depth continues to hover in the 1100-1500 foot
range. No bucket tips as of yet, but cannot rule out some patchy
drizzle along the immediate coast. Coverage and confidence isn`t
high enough to officially include drizzle in the forecast this
morning.

Other weather highlights for Wednesday include temperatures and
wind. The dropping upper level low is ushering in some colder
850mb temperatures. Colder temperatures aloft for September
standard. As such, the airmass will support high temperatures in
the 60s at the coast and 70s with a few low 80s inland. While it
may feel nice, those values are 5 to 10 degrees below normal. The
colder airmass aloft and surface pressure gradient orientations
continue to promote gusty onshore flow this afternoon. Forecast
captures this well and highlights gusty conditions along the
coast, inland valleys, and inland gaps/passes (specifically
Altamont/Pacheco Passes). The windiest spots could see gusts up
40-45 mph. Winds will ease late in the day and more so overnight
as the onshore gradient relaxes.

For Wednesday night: given the lower 500mb heights overhead the
marine layer will deep further and exceed 2000 feet with a run
toward 2500-3000 feet by Thursday morning. Deep marine layer,
onshore flow, and enhanced orographic lift will lead to higher
confidence for widespread coastal drizzle. Despite the deeper
marine layer it`s not looking like a nocturnal drying set up,
which is good from a fire weather perspective.

